reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/database-repair/verification.json" "basis" 653} {"question" "q-72336" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948006/state.json" "file_sha256" "a546006f34867d121c7e5670d5d3de2f8e7d622cd5e0ed87e29b672e0d99d644" "basis" 582 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948006/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-72336" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{-3}}{4{x}^{-5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948006/state.json" "file_sha256" "a546006f34867d121c7e5670d5d3de2f8e7d622cd5e0ed87e29b672e0d99d644" "basis" 582 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948006/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-72336" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948006/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f338d77ee01746b9a0b348615cc7f4946f6911bf5686ee